---
.title = "Object-capability RPC for OCapN, in Zig",
.description = "pancake is a Zig library for OCapN and CapTP: object-capability RPC with no ambient authority to fall back on.",
.date = @date("2026-06-12T00:00:00"),
.author = "Jim Isaacs",
.layout = "index.shtml",
.draft = false,
---

Two programs pass each other references to objects and call methods across the
network, under the same rule as local code. A peer can call only the references
it has been handed, and it cannot construct one it was not given. That is
[OCapN](https://github.com/ocapn/ocapn). pancake is a
[Zig](https://ziglang.org/) library that speaks it.

Most network authorization works the other way around. You prove who you are,
the service looks up what you are allowed to do, and anything you hand your
credentials to inherits all of it. With references there is nothing to inherit:
a program gets the objects you passed it and has no way to name anything else.
[Why OCapN]($link.page('why')) explains the difference using protocols you
already run.

[Their program hands yours a reference to an object called "greeter." Holding that reference is the whole permission to call it.]($image.siteAsset('diagram-capability.svg'))

```zig
const session = try pancake.driver.connect(
    allocator,
    &.{tcp_resident.netlayer()},
    partner,
    .ephemeral,
    .{ .handshake = .{ .ms = 10_000 }, .settle = .{ .ms = 10_000 } },
);
const greeter = try session.fetch("greeter"); // an opaque capability, by name
const settled = try session.call(greeter, "greet", &.{.{ .string = "world" }}, arena.allocator());
```

That is the conversation: dial, fetch a capability by name, call it. The
allocators and the transport around it are a few more lines.
[Start building]($link.page('start')) walks all of them in Zig, then points at
the same entry in C and each binding.

pancake is not released yet. Pin a commit, and expect the API to move.
[What you can depend on]($link.page('stability')) separates checked behavior
from surfaces that still need outside evidence.

The protocol core does no I/O of its own. It is a synchronous state machine you
feed bytes into and drain bytes out of, so sockets, threads, and scheduling stay
in your program where you can see them. Peers can meet over Tor
([onion]($link.page('transports/onion'))), sealed TCP
([Noise]($link.page('transports/noise'))), encrypted discovery
([iroh]($link.page('transports/iroh'))), or a browser DataChannel
([WebRTC]($link.page('transports/webrtc'))), among other paths.
[Choosing a transport]($link.page('transports/choosing')) is the decision table.

Hold the library as a Zig package, a plain C ABI, or a native binding (Rust,
TypeScript, Python, Racket). The TypeScript binding also compiles to
WebAssembly, so the same session runs in the browser. A
[CapTP CLI]($link.page('cli')) sits beside those surfaces: `zig build` puts the
`pancake` binary in `zig-out/bin`.

If the ideas are new, start at [Why OCapN]($link.page('why')). If you are ready
to dial, go to [Start building]($link.page('start')).
